21875, 0.0111846923828125, 0.013916015625, 0.003574371337890625, -0.04107666015625, 0.0170135498046875, 0.00885009765625, -0.011444091796875, 0.0235595703125, 0.032379150390625, 0.0135345458984375, -0.015655517578125, 0.0194854736328125, 0.0141448974609375, 0.006694793701171875, -0.0055694580078125, -0.0238800048828125, -0.0150909423828125, -0.01898193359375, -0.0513916015625, -0.007366180419921875, -0.017822265625, -0.01073455810546875, 0.006420135498046875, -0.0261993408203125, 0.0180206298828125, -0.0038604736328125, 0.010162353515625, 0.01461029052734375, 0.035797119140625, 0.03961181640625, -0.0243988037109375, 0.0178680419921875, 0.03955078125, 0.02288818359375, 0.007843017578125, 0.0177154541015625, -0.0206756591796875, -0.0088653564453125, 0.00534820556640625, -0.00014460086822509766, -0.01041412353515625, 0.0090789794921875, 0.014007568359375, -0.011993408203125, -0.01131439208984375, 0.0098114013671875, 0.0024776458740234375, 0.0005745887756347656, 0.0061798095703125, -0.0121002197265625, -0.007480621337890625, -0.01436614990234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Democracy is a contested concept, and how it is defined has significant implications for both academic research and policymaking. This document argues that the choice between minimalist (procedural) and maximalist (substantive) definitions of democracy fundamentally shapes not only scholarly understanding but also practical assessments and policy decisions regarding political systems. Specifically, it demonstrates how definitional choices\u2014whether democracy is defined narrowly by its procedures or more broadly to include substantive rights and freedoms\u2014directly affect how regimes are evaluated, compared, and engaged with worldwide.\n\n**Defining Democracy: Minimalist (Procedural) vs. Maximalist (Substantive) Approaches**\n\nA **minimalist (procedural) definition** of democracy focuses on the existence of regular, competitive, and inclusive elections that allow citizens to choose their leaders. Joseph Schumpeter (1942) famously argued that democracy is best understood as an institutional arrangement for arriving at political decisions through a competitive struggle for the people\u2019s vote. In this view, democracy is defined by clear, observable procedures\u2014universal suffrage, secret ballots, and the peaceful transfer of power\u2014rather than by outcomes or broader social context.\n\nIn contrast, a **maximalist (substantive) definition** incorporates not only electoral procedures but also a broader set of requirements, such as the protection of civil liberties, human rights, and the rule of law. Robert Dahl (1971) introduced the concept of \"polyarchy\" to describe real-world approximations of democracy, emphasizing both contestation (competition among elites) and participation (inclusiveness of the electorate). Polyarchy requires not just free and fair elections but also freedoms of speech, assembly, and association, as well as equal legal protection. Maximalist criteria thus include both democratic practices (e.g., competitive elections, independent judiciary) and democratic values (e.g., political equality, protection of minority rights).\n\n**From Theory to Practice: Why Definitions Matter**\n\nThe way democracy is defined is not merely theoretical; it has practical consequences for how countries are classified and how international actors engage with them. Before turning to specific case studies, it is important to clarify how procedural and substantive criteria are applied in practice. Under minimalist definitions, the focus is on whether elections are held and whether they are competitive and inclusive. Substantive definitions, by contrast, require additional evidence of civil liberties, rule of law, and protection of rights.\n\n**Systematic Comparison: Case Studies**\n\nA systematic comparison of cases illustrates the implications of these definitions. In each case, we specify which features correspond to procedural (minimalist) and substantive (maximalist) criteria:\n\n- **Russia**: Regular elections are held (procedural/minimalist criterion), but these elections are widely criticized for lacking genuine competition and for significant barriers to opposition participation (OSCE/ODIHR, 2021). Severe restrictions on media and civil society (substantive/maximalist criteria) further undermine the substantive aspects of democracy (Freedom House 2022). Thus, while Russia may appear to meet minimalist criteria on the surface, the lack of meaningful competition and procedural integrity calls even this into question.\n- **Turkey**: Elections are held regularly (procedural), but increasing restrictions on media, civil society, and opposition parties (substantive) erode substantive rights (Human Rights Watch 2022; Freedom House 2022). Freedom House (2022) notes, \"The government\u2019s control over the judiciary and media has undermined checks and balances.\" Under a minimalist definition, Turkey might be classified as democratic, but under a maximalist definition, it falls short.\n- **India**: Known for vibrant elections and peaceful transfers of power (procedural), yet ongoing concerns about civil liberties and minority rights\u2014such as restrictions on NGOs and communal violence (substantive)\u2014challenge its status as a substantive democracy (Amnesty International 2021; Human Rights Watch 2022). For instance, Human Rights Watch (2022) documents \"increasing harassment of journalists and activists.\"\n- **Sweden**: Meets both procedural and substantive criteria, with competitive elections (procedural), strong civil liberties, and high rankings in global indices for press freedom and civil rights (substantive) (Reporters Without Borders 2022; Freedom House 2022).\n\nThese examples demonstrate how the choice of definition can lead to divergent assessments of the same country and reveal gray areas where regimes may meet some but not all criteria. This underscores the central thesis: definitional choices shape both scholarly and policy judgments.\n\n**Critiques and Counterarguments**\n\nThe debate between minimalist and maximalist definitions is longstanding and complex. Minimalist definitions are often criticized, notably by Larry Diamond and advocates of liberal democracy, for ignoring substantive elements such as the protection of rights and the inclusiveness of participation. They argue that focusing solely on procedures risks legitimizing regimes that are democratic in form but not in substance. For example, labeling Russia or Turkey as democracies based solely on elections overlooks significant violations of rights and freedoms (Diamond 2002).\n\nHowever, proponents of minimalism, such as Adam Przeworski, counter that including a wide range of civil liberties and social rights makes the concept of democracy too broad and difficult to operationalize. Przeworski (2000) argues that democracy should be defined by clear, observable procedures to allow for objective comparison and to avoid subjective judgments about rights, which can vary across contexts. Minimalist definitions also have practical advantages: they are easier to measure, less prone to cultural bias, and more transparent in their criteria.\n\nCritics of maximalism note that the measurement of rights can be subjective and context-dependent, complicating comparative research and leading to disagreements over classification. For example, the classification of \"hybrid regimes\" or \"illiberal democracies\" (Levitsky & Way 2010) highlights the challenge of regimes that combine procedural elections with substantive deficits. Measurement challenges are evident in the varying scores assigned by Freedom House and the Economist Intelligence Unit to countries like Hungary, reflecting different emphases on procedural versus substantive criteria. There is also debate about whether some rights (e.g., social or economic rights) should be considered essential to democracy or are better seen as outcomes of democratic governance. Some scholars (e.g., Munck 2009) argue that maximalist definitions risk conflating democracy with good governance or liberalism, potentially obscuring important distinctions.\n\n**Policy Implications and Real-World Consequences**\n\nThe distinction between minimalist and maximalist definitions is not merely academic; it has concrete policy implications. International organizations such as Freedom House and the Economist Intelligence Unit use different criteria to rate countries, which can influence foreign aid decisions, diplomatic recognition, and the design of democracy promotion programs. For example, after the Arab Spring, some transitional governments received international support based on their adoption of electoral procedures (procedural/minimalist), even as civil liberties remained restricted (Freedom House 2012). In Tunisia, for instance, international aid increased following the introduction of competitive elections, despite ongoing concerns about press freedom and judicial independence (Freedom House 2012; World Bank 2013). Similarly, the classification of Hungary as a \"flawed democracy\" by the Economist Intelligence Unit, due to declining press freedom and judicial independence, has affected EU funding and diplomatic relations (Economist Intelligence Unit 2022; European Commission 2022). According to the EIU (2022), Hungary\u2019s score dropped from 6.72 in 2015 to 6.50 in 2022, reflecting substantive declines. These cases show how definitional choices can lead to policy missteps, such as supporting regimes that meet procedural but not substantive standards, or withholding support from countries making progress on rights but lacking perfect electoral procedures. It is important to note, however, that the causal impact of democracy ratings on aid or recognition is often mediated by other factors, and evidence is sometimes illustrative rather than definitive (Dietrich & Wright 2015).\n\n**Conclusion and Recommendations**\n\nIn conclusion, the debate over how to define democracy is central to both scholarship and policy. Definitional clarity is essential for meaningful comparative analysis, effective democracy promotion, and informed judgments about political systems. Scholars and policymakers should be transparent about their criteria, recognize the trade-offs involved in different approaches, and be attentive to the gray areas between procedural and substantive dimensions.\n\nActionable recommendations include: (1) explicitly stating and justifying definitional choices in research and policy documents, for example by including a dedicated section on definitions and criteria; (2) using multiple indicators\u2014such as combining the Varieties of Democracy (V-Dem) electoral democracy index with Freedom House civil liberties scores and World Justice Project rule of law measures\u2014to capture both procedural and substantive aspects of democracy; and (3) ensuring transparency by publishing the sources, coding rules, and data underlying democracy assessments, and by regularly reviewing and updating these criteria in light of new evidence. For example, organizations can make their coding manuals and datasets publicly available and invite external audits or peer review. Ultimately, understanding the diversity of democratic practices and values worldwide requires careful attention to both procedures and rights, supported by precise definitions and systematic analysis. At the same time, researchers and policymakers should recognize the practical advantages of minimalist definitions\u2014such as ease of measurement and cross-national comparability\u2014while remaining aware of their limitations in capturing the full range of democratic quality.\n\n**References**\n\n- Schumpeter, J. A. (1942). *Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy*. New York: Harper & Brothers.\n- Dahl, R. A. (1971). *Polyarchy: Participation and Opposition*. New Haven: Yale University Press.\n- Przeworski, A., Alvarez, M. E., Cheibub, J. A., & Limongi, F. (2000). *Democracy and Development: Political Institutions and Well-Being in the World, 1950\u20131990*. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.\n- Levitsky, S., & Way, L. A. (2010). *Competitive Authoritarianism: Hybrid Regimes after the Cold War*. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.\n- Diamond,"}}